                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="1340-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_c.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Cardioid" title="Cardioid" ></span>Sennheiser Electronics Corporation e 602 II</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Cardioid Dynamic Microphone</h2>
                    <p>The e 602 II is a large-diaphragm dynamic mic designed for bass instruments such as kick drum, floor tom, bass cab, and tuba. It is the revision to Sennheiser&#8217;s original e602.</p><p>Both versions are Cardioid dynamics with a &#8220;mid-scoop&#8221; EQ curve, intended for bass instrument reproduction. The primary differences between the two models are summarized below:</p><table style="width:auto"><tr><td></td><td><b>e 602</b></td><td><b>e 602 II</b></td></tr><tr><td>Weight:</td><td>550g</td><td>318g</td></tr><tr><td>Impedance:</td><td>250&Omega;</td><td>350&Omega;</td></tr><tr><td>HF boost:</td><td>+8dB, 2&ndash;15kHz</td><td>+16dB, 5.5&ndash;12kHz</td></tr><tr><td>LF Peak:</td><td>80Hz</td><td>60Hz</td></tr></table><p>The lighter weight is due to the use of an aluminum body; the change was intentional, to make the mic easier to mount at the end of a boom arm reaching into a kick drum. </p><p>The II presents a much deeper mid-frequency scoop than does the original mic, with a narrower and higher-amplitude HF boost. The low-frequency boost in the II is, conversely, broader, with deeper LF extension.</p><p>Both versions of the microphone are only nominally Cardioid; in both cases, the polar pattern becomes omnidirectional at low frequencies. The rear rejection on the original e602 is somewhat better than on the II, although when placed inside a kick drum this should not affect performance in any meaningful way.</p><p>Both mics have an integrated stand mount. Both use a humbucking coil to reduce EMI/RFI noise.</p><blockquote><p><cite>Electronic Musicician 2011</cite><br />I christened the E602 the Great Equalizer (pun intended) due to its predictable, instantly identifiable response that coaxes from every drum a quite similar, over-the-top sound. The E602’s preponderance of lows largely obscures midrange shell tone, minimizing the distinctive voice of each drum in favor of a singular, killer tone. That’s not a bad thing if it’s consistency and reliability you’re after.</p></blockquote><p><p>The <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/Sennheiser/e-602-II">Sennheiser Electronics Corporation e 602 II</a> is also known as: e602-II.</p>
